   "text": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# See which process held a local TCP port\n\nportlog records port ownership over time, so you can look it up after a process exits.\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nmacOS and Linux · Written in Go · MIT license\n\n## Look back at a port\n\n“What was on port 3000 an hour ago?”\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\nSee every recorded process that held port 3000, newest first.\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `portlog start` | Start the background recorder. |\n| `portlog stop` | Stop the recorder. |\n| `portlog who 3000 --since 2h` | Limit port 3000’s history to the last two hours. |\n| `portlog free 3000` | Stop the process holding port 3000 now, after confirmation. |\n\nUse `portlog free 3000 -y` to skip confirmation.\n\n## Good to know\n\n**History**  \nRecords stay in `~/.portlog/` for 7 days.\n\n**Command-line arguments**  \nportlog never records command-line arguments, because they can hold secrets.\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What does portlog do?\n(b) What do you run first after installing it?\n(c) Can it show a process that already exited?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (c) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
